# Colossus

Colossus was a series of computers developed by British codebreakers during World War II to cryptanalyze the Lorenz cipher. Designed by General Post Office (GPO) telephone engineer Tommy Flowers, the first machine, Colossus Mark 1, became operational at Bletchley Park in February 1944. It is widely recognized by historians as the world's first programmable, electronic, digital computer, although it was programmed via physical switches and plugboards rather than a stored software program. Colossus utilized thousands of vacuum tubes (thermionic valves) to perform Boolean logic operations at electronic speeds, massively accelerating the decryption of high-level strategic communications between the German High Command and their field commanders. Because the project was classified under the highest levels of state secrecy, the machines were systematically dismantled after the war, and their existence was hidden from the global public for decades. Consequently, Colossus was deprived of its rightful place in the early history of computing, allowing later American machines like the ENIAC to claim many of its historical firsts. Today, a fully functional replica operates at The National Museum of Computing at Bletchley Park, standing as a testament to the monumental engineering achievement that significantly shortened the Second World War.

---

## The Lorenz Cipher and Max Newman

While the German Enigma machine is the most famous encryption device of World War II, the German High Command utilized a significantly more complex teleprinter cipher machine—the Lorenz SZ40/42—for their most sensitive strategic communications. The British codebreakers at Bletchley Park referred to the Lorenz machine and its traffic as "Tunny."

The mathematician Bill Tutte successfully deduced the complete logical structure of the Lorenz machine in 1941 without ever seeing the physical device, merely by analyzing intercepted traffic. Following this breakthrough, Max Newman, a mathematician at Bletchley Park, proposed automating the cryptanalysis of the Lorenz messages using electronic counting machines. Newman's initial machine, the "Heath Robinson," utilized electromechanical relays and dual synchronized paper tapes. However, it was slow, notoriously unreliable, and prone to tearing the paper tape due to the mechanical synchronization required.

## Tommy Flowers and Electronic Architecture

Tommy Flowers, a brilliant telecommunications engineer at the Post Office Research Station at Dollis Hill, was brought in to assist with the Heath Robinson. Flowers realized that a completely different architecture was required. He proposed building a massive machine utilizing thermionic valves (vacuum tubes) for all internal logic and counting operations, a radical concept at the time, as valves were widely considered too fragile and unreliable for large-scale digital computing.

Flowers argued that if the valves were left continuously powered on, they would not burn out from thermal shock. Working independently and using his own funds for some of the initial development, Flowers and his team engineered the Colossus Mark 1 in eleven months.

### Processing and Paper Tape

The architectural genius of Colossus lay in its elimination of the dual-tape synchronization problem that plagued the Heath Robinson. Colossus read the encrypted message from a single continuous loop of paper tape using an optical reader. Instead of running a second physical tape containing the generated Lorenz wheel patterns, Colossus simulated the Lorenz wheels electronically using arrays of thyratron rings.

The paper tape was driven via a sophisticated pulley system at 30 miles per hour, allowing the photoelectric reader to scan 5,000 characters per second. The electronic logic circuits, programmed by configuring cables on a massive plugboard, performed rapid Boolean operations (such as XOR) against the incoming data and the simulated wheel patterns to deduce the starting positions of the Lorenz machine.

---

> **Key insight:** By replacing physical mechanical parts with purely electronic thyratron rings, Colossus operated at speeds that were entirely unmatched by any other calculating device of the era.

---

## Operational Impact

The Colossus Mark 1 was delivered to Bletchley Park in January 1944 and successfully performed its first attack on a Lorenz message in early February. Its success was absolute, reducing the time required to break a Lorenz message from weeks to merely hours.

An upgraded version, the Colossus Mark 2, utilized 2,400 vacuum tubes and featured five parallel processors, dramatically increasing its operational speed. The first Mark 2 went into service on June 1, 1944, just days before the Normandy landings (D-Day). The intelligence provided by Colossus—codenamed Ultra—confirmed to Allied commanders that the German High Command had swallowed the deception campaigns and falsely believed the invasion would occur at Pas de Calais. By the end of the war, ten Colossus machines were operating continuously at Bletchley Park.

## Secrecy, Destruction, and Reconstruction

Following the conclusion of World War II, Winston Churchill ordered that the Colossus machines be dismantled and broken into pieces no larger than a man's fist to preserve the absolute secrecy of Britain's cryptanalytic capabilities. Tommy Flowers was ordered to burn all blueprints and documentation related to the machine's construction. Two machines were retained by British intelligence (GCHQ) for training and auxiliary work, but they too were destroyed in the 1960s.

Because of the Official Secrets Act, those who engineered and operated Colossus could not claim credit for their monumental achievements during their lifetimes. The existence of the machines was not declassified until the mid-1970s.

In 1993, Tony Sale, a British computer conservationist, launched a project to rebuild a fully functioning Colossus Mark 2 based on fragmented surviving notes, photographs, and interviews with surviving engineers. Completed in 2007, the working replica is permanently housed at The National Museum of Computing on the Bletchley Park estate.

